<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="RkYekgNOrewR8JYrVZBLef" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="OpJwiHZV4uuY1mjxq3xL3a" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Q7EK6dknPcaCER6IMV4uJE" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="HHi2VM4mbXtc4zGFuM255H" Location="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="VONhWAYwDc3szcvzGG4eeE" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="HRU7D1rjsAmGR4t4Fwzi2B">
    <Canvas Id="UlcUpKvSduqXtBDh6auwwK" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="DxMI55PZXECZKO2yoShdc1">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="BOLAEIVE82m6rlh1Sqh4fN">
        <Canvas Id="CoHMcSvwNk8MmekDzShSB8" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="BpN4nVaipLfjCmlfUUHHGX" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1640" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="13 - Close does not mean reachable.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A small town, typed by hand. A river through the middle, one bridge. An orange dot on the north&#xD;&#xA;bank, and your cursor. Two lines join them: a thin grey one, straight, and a thick teal one that&#xD;&#xA;goes the long way round. Two numbers say how long each is.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PUT THE CURSOR ON THE SOUTH BANK, DIRECTLY BELOW THE DOT. Straight: about 200 m. Along the&#xD;&#xA;streets: 800 m - to the bridge, over, and back. Same two places. Four times the distance.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NOW CLICK BRIDGE CLOSED. The teal line disappears. FOUND says False. Not zero metres - no&#xD;&#xA;metres: there is no route, and the patch says so instead of drawing something. The grey line&#xD;&#xA;has not moved a pixel. Two places 200 metres apart, and you cannot get from one to the other.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THAT IS THE CHAPTER. Distance, topology and routing are three different things:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  DISTANCE   how far apart two places are        - the grey line. Geometry.&#xD;&#xA;  TOPOLOGY   what is connected to what           - the streets, and only where they share an&#xD;&#xA;                                                    endpoint. A fact about the data.&#xD;&#xA;  ROUTING    the shortest way through the topology - the teal line. Arithmetic on top.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Chapter 04's Nearest Points answered the first. This chapter answers the third, and the&#xD;&#xA;second is what makes them different.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT COUNTS AS CONNECTED. Look at the river: it crosses the bridge, in this picture, at a&#xD;&#xA;right angle. It is not connected to it. Nothing crosses anything in a network unless the data&#xD;&#xA;SAYS so, and here it says so in exactly one way: two streets share an endpoint with identical&#xD;&#xA;coordinates. Not close - identical. Not crossing - sharing an end. A line that passes over&#xD;&#xA;another is a bridge or an overpass until the data says it is a junction, and geometry alone&#xD;&#xA;cannot tell you which.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS TOWN IS IN METRES, AND WE DECIDED THAT. Chapter 10 said a number has no spatial meaning&#xD;&#xA;until you know its coordinate system. Here is the positive version: this town's coordinate&#xD;&#xA;system is a local flat one where one unit IS one metre, because we said so at the top of the&#xD;&#xA;patch - and so Length really is metres. No longitude anywhere. No map. A map would put the&#xD;&#xA;town somewhere; this chapter is about how it is joined up, not where it is.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE SNAP DISTANCE. Your cursor is not on a street corner. ShortestPath moves it to the&#xD;&#xA;nearest one before it routes, and TO SNAP DISTANCE tells you by how much - so you always know&#xD;&#xA;the route did not start exactly where you pointed. Park on a corner and it reads 0.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NETWORKS BUILT. Reads 1, then 2 when you close the bridge, 3 when you open it. Closing the&#xD;&#xA;bridge hands BuildNetwork a different set of streets, and a different set of streets is a&#xD;&#xA;different town - so it builds a new one, once, and says so. The path is recomputed every&#xD;&#xA;frame and is not counted, because on a town this size it costs nothing; the topology is what&#xD;&#xA;is worth keeping.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Add a second bridge. Type one more line into the WITH BRIDGE box - '(300 -100, 300 100)' -&#xD;&#xA;  and watch the route from the far east change its mind.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Break a street by one metre. Change a shared endpoint from 0 100 to 0 101 in one of the two&#xD;&#xA;  lines that meet there. Node Count goes up by one, and the route around it is gone. Close&#xD;&#xA;  does not mean connected either.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Make the river a street. Add its line to the WKT: it crosses the bridge, and still nothing&#xD;&#xA;  connects, because crossing is not a shared endpoint. Then run both through Union first (it&#xD;&#xA;  is in NTS.Operation) - now the crossing IS a junction. That is a claim you just made about&#xD;&#xA;  the world, not a fact the geometry knew: real bridges cross real rivers without a ramp.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  network / graph      places and the connections between them&#xD;&#xA;  node                 a place where connections meet - here, a street's endpoint&#xD;&#xA;  edge                 one connection - here, one street, with its length as the cost&#xD;&#xA;  connectivity         which nodes you can reach from which. Nothing to do with distance&#xD;&#xA;  network distance     length along the edges, as opposed to as the crow flies&#xD;&#xA;  shortest path        the cheapest sequence of edges between two nodes. Dijkstra, 1956&#xD;&#xA;  noding               splitting lines where they cross so the crossing becomes a node.&#xD;&#xA;                       A modelling decision, not a geometric one&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  AND THE HONEST GAP: this network is EXPERIMENTAL and says so in its category name.&#xD;&#xA;  NetTopologySuite has no shortest-path algorithm; these two nodes were written for this&#xD;&#xA;  chapter and live under NTS.Experimental until it is clear whether a real, reusable network&#xD;&#xA;  abstraction wants to exist. No one-way streets, no turn costs, no travel time, no OSM - a&#xD;&#xA;  real road network brings a dozen contracts of its own, and this chapter is about one.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  Pathfinding. Game navigation meshes, A* on a grid, node graphs in a patch editor. You have&#xD;&#xA;  met this idea the day you first made something walk round a wall. The only new thing here is&#xD;&#xA;  that the graph came out of geometry, and the geometry is a town.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS PATCH USES ONE PACKAGE, VL.NetTopologySuite. A unit needing one package normally belongs&#xD;&#xA;in that package's own help folder; the exemption is written into this pack's rules for the&#xD;&#xA;spine, and the sequence needs connectivity here, after distance and inside, before the world&#xD;&#xA;is real data. No map, on purpose, see above.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 04              the grey line, alone: the shortest line between, without streets&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 11              the other kind of asking-many: who is nearby, not who is reachable&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 09              real data - and real streets come as data, with all their contracts">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <!-- ===== A. the town, in metres ===== -->
          <Pad Id="GB9qwi8zutsODAnukFvx8s" Bounds="60,-60,700,45" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="THE TOWN. A local flat coordinate system: ONE UNIT IS ONE METRE, by our decision, which is what makes&#xD;&#xA;Length below honestly metres. Streets are one MultiLineString; two streets are connected only where they&#xD;&#xA;share an endpoint with IDENTICAL coordinates. The river is drawn, and is not in the network at all.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="LKzv8mmCZClTmNv0lXmFKl" Comment="WITH BRIDGE" Bounds="60,0,760,20" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="MULTILINESTRING ((-300 100, 0 100), (0 100, 300 100), (300 100, 300 250), (-300 -100, 0 -100), (0 -100, 300 -100), (-300 -100, -300 -250), (0 -100, 0 100))">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="SpvUUJYxUkzb2rUtDHxfvc" Comment="BRIDGE CLOSED - the same town without the last line" Bounds="60,30,760,20" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="MULTILINESTRING ((-300 100, 0 100), (0 100, 300 100), (300 100, 300 250), (-300 -100, 0 -100), (0 -100, 300 -100), (-300 -100, -300 -250))">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
